 3. THE PUBLIC FORK CONDITIONS
================================================================================

   A Fork is a Public Fork, and You may keep and distribute it, only for as
   long as ALL of the following are true.

   3.1  Public availability.
        The complete source code of the Fork, with its full history of
        Modifications, is available to everyone, at no charge, without
        registration, authentication, approval, agreement to additional terms,
        geographic or organizational restriction, or any other barrier, on a
        public hosting platform that allows anyone to browse and clone it.
        You may not remove, hide, archive-and-delete, rename-to-evade, or
        otherwise make a Public Fork unavailable while You keep distributing
        any copy or any binary or deployment derived from it.

   3.2  Same License, unmodified.
        The Fork carries this License, in full and unmodified, as its LICENSE
        file at the repository root, and every Modification You make is
        licensed to everyone under this same License (Section 11) and nothing
        else. You may not add terms that further restrict what this License
        permits, and You may not apply any other license to Your Modifications
        as a whole. Your own purely original files that are independent of the
        Software may carry their own license, but the Fork as a whole remains
        under this License.

   3.3  Notices and credit.
        You keep every copyright notice, license notice, attribution notice,
        provenance header and NOTICE file in the Software. You add the
        Schedule B notice to the first screen of the Fork's README, and You
        keep a visible link to the Licensor's repository.

   3.4  Marked changes.
        You mark every file that You modified, with a prominent statement that
        You changed it and the date of the change. You keep a changelog or a
        commit history that shows what You changed. You do not present
        Your Modifications as the Licensor's work.

   3.5  Own name.
        The Fork carries a name that is clearly different from the Exponet
        Marks (Section 6) and states that it is an unofficial Fork of
        ExponetDB that is neither made nor endorsed by the Licensor.

   3.6  Non-commercial, still.
        The Fork, its distribution and the way You use it remain free of
        Commercial Use. You do not charge for the Fork, do not gate any part of
        it behind payment, do not sell support, hosting or services based on
        it, do not monetize its repository page, documentation or releases
        with advertising or affiliate links, and do not condition anything on
        a purchase.

   3.7  No Production Use through the Fork.
        Publishing a Fork does not give You, or anyone who gets the Fork, a
        right to Production Use or to Commercial Use. Those require Written
        Permission or a Commercial License from the Licensor, for every person
        who wants them. You say so in Your README (Schedule B).

   3.8  Upstream grant for Public Fork Modifications.
        For every Modification that You publish in a Public Fork, You grant
        the Licensor a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide, non-exclusive,
        royalty-free, fully paid-up, transferable and sublicensable (through
        multiple tiers) license to use, reproduce, modify, prepare derivative
        works of, publicly display, publicly perform, distribute and otherwise
        exploit that Modification and any Derivative Work of it, in source and
        object form, under any license terms the Licensor chooses, including
        commercial and proprietary terms and a Commercial License, together
        with a license under the patent claims You own that are necessarily
        infringed by that Modification alone or in combination with the
        Software. You warrant that You have the right to grant this license.
        This clause does not transfer ownership of Your Modifications to the
        Licensor, and it does not oblige the Licensor to use them.

   3.9  Compliance on request.
        If the Licensor tells You, in writing, that a Fork does not meet this
        Section 3, You cure the problem within thirty (30) days (Section 12.3)
        or take the Fork down.

   3.10 Pull requests and issues.
        Nothing in this Section stops You from sending Your Modifications back
        to the Licensor as Contributions under Section 8.


