 5. COMMERCIAL USE, WRITTEN PERMISSION AND COMMERCIAL LICENSES
================================================================================

   5.1  Default: you have to ask.
        This License does not allow Production Use or Commercial Use. If You
        want either, You must contact the Licensor first and obtain a Written
        Permission (5.2) or a Commercial License (5.4) BEFORE You start.
        Starting first and asking later does not cure the breach; it only
        affects the Licensor's choice under Section 12.

   5.2  Written Permission for non-commercial Production Use.
        For individuals, schools, universities, research groups and
        non-profit organizations who want Production Use that is not Commercial
        Use, the Licensor may grant a Written Permission, free of charge or
        for a fee, at the Licensor's discretion, limited in scope and in term,
        and revocable for future use as the permission states.

   5.3  No obligation.
        The Licensor has no duty to grant any permission or license, to give a
        reason, to answer within any time, to offer any price, or to treat
        similar requests alike. The Licensor may grant a permission or a
        license to one person and refuse it to another.

   5.4  Commercial Licenses.
        A Commercial License is a separate written agreement that the Licensor
        and You sign or accept in text form. It sets the scope, the field of
        use, the number of installations or environments, the term, the fees,
        the support and the rest. A Commercial License is the only way to
        obtain the right to Commercial Use. Its terms replace this License for
        the rights it covers, and only for those rights. A Commercial License
        is personal to the licensee and not transferable without the Licensor's
        written consent.

   5.5  Evaluation of a Commercial License.
        If You want to evaluate whether to buy a Commercial License, ask for
        an evaluation permission. The Licensor may grant a short, limited
        evaluation permission in Written Permission form.

   5.6  Pricing and negotiation.
        Prices and terms are agreed case by case. A request is not a
        commitment by either side until a Written Permission or a Commercial
        License is signed or accepted.

   5.7  Past use.
        If You used the Software in a way that needed a Commercial License and
        did not have one, You contact the Licensor promptly. The Licensor may
        offer a retroactive license for a fee, may require You to stop, or may
        pursue the remedies that the law gives the Licensor. Nothing here
        waives any claim.


