 SCHEDULE C. EXAMPLES (NON-EXHAUSTIVE, FOR INTERPRETATION ONLY)
================================================================================

   C.1  Permitted:

        * You read the migration engine to learn how plan, apply and verify
          fit together, and You write a blog post that quotes ten lines of it
          with a link to the repository.
        * You fork the repository on GitHub, fix a bug in Your public fork,
          open a pull request, run the test suite on Your laptop against
          throw-away containers, and leave Your fork public.
        * You find a vulnerability in a local lab, tell the Licensor privately,
          and publish an analysis after the fix.
        * You use an AI assistant to explain a module of the code to You.

   C.2  Not permitted without Written Permission or a Commercial License:

        * Your agency installs ExponetDB to manage a client's PostgreSQL
          servers, even if you charge nothing for ExponetDB itself.
        * Your startup runs ExponetDB internally to manage its production
          database, even if no customer ever sees it.
        * You run ExponetDB at home on a server that stays on, to manage the
          databases of Your hobby project that real people use.
        * You publish a hosted "ExponetDB Cloud" or "managed Exponet" offer,
          or a one-click deploy template.
        * You keep a private fork in Your company's internal Git server.
        * You take the schemas and the protocol documents and build a competing
          product from them.
        * You copy the repository into a training corpus.
        * You remove the license header and rename the product.

   C.3  Needs a decision by the Licensor (ask first):

        * A university lab wants to run ExponetDB for a course for a semester.
        * A non-profit wants to manage the databases of its volunteers.
        * A company wants a paid pilot.
        * A conference talk wants to demo it live on stage.


================================================================================
 SCHEDULE D. PLAIN-LANGUAGE ANSWERS TO COMMON QUESTIONS (NON-BINDING)
================================================================================

   Is this open source?
     No. The source is visible and forkable in public, but running it in
     production or commercially needs the Licensor's permission. Open Source
     Initiative definitions and "free software" definitions do not apply to
     this License.

   Can I try it out?
     You can run it on your own computer to develop and test a public fork or a
     contribution, against fake data. For anything beyond that, ask first.

   Can I use it at work?
     Not without a commercial license. "At work" is Commercial Use even if
     nobody pays for the software.

   Can I use it for my side project?
     If the side project is real (real users, real data, or any chance of
     income), ask first. A written permission for hobby projects may be easy to
     get.

   Why do forks have to stay public?
     So that improvements remain visible to everyone, and so that nobody builds
     a closed product on the work. The Licensor gets a license to forks'
     modifications (Section 3.8) to be able to include them upstream and in a
     Commercial License.

   Can I send a pull request?
     Yes. See Section 8 and CONTRIBUTING.md.

   Can I get a commercial license?
     Yes. Write to the address in Section 20 and see Schedule A.

   Is this legal advice?
     No. This License is a legal text written by the Licensor. If a lot depends
     on it for You, ask a lawyer of Your own.


